s of factories, it at once banished lively interest from a stranger's mind and left only a dull oppression of the spirit.", "reference": "1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter VII, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": ["cognition", "learning"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Continual prayer on a dead person's behalf for a period after their death.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": ["a month's [or monthly] mind; a year's mind"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(uncountable) Attention, consideration or thought.", "tags": ["uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "They are the “tars” who give mind to the spreading sail, and their bold courage is the pabulum which will preserve our sea-girt isle in its vernal green to furthest posterity.", "reference": "1849, Eliza Cook, Eliza Cook’s Journal,p.119, volume 1:"}, {"text": "Then he, having mind of Beelzebub, the god of flies, fled without a halt homewards; but, falling in the coo's loan, broke two ribs and a collar bone, the whilk misfortune was much blessed to his soul.", "reference": "1902, John Buchan, The Outgoing of the Tide:"}, {"text": "If you get a “trolling” comment, delete it, do not respond to it, and move forward immediately without paying any further mind.", "reference": "2014, Jolie O'Dell, Blogging for Photographers, page 66:"}], "synonyms": ["heed", "mark", "notice", "advertence", "advertency", "attention", "consideration", "heed", "heedfulness", "mark", "mind", "note", "notice", "observance", "observation", "reflection", "regard"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/maɪnd/", "tags": ["General American", "Received Pronunciation"]}], "forms": [{"word": "minds", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "minding", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["past"]}, {"word": "no-table-tags", "tags": ["table-tags"]}, {"word": "glossary", "tags": ["inflection-template"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["infinitive"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["first-person", "present", "singular"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["first-person", "past", "singular"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["present", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "mindest", "tags": ["archaic", "present", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["past", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "mindedst", "tags": ["archaic", "past", "second-person", "singular"]}, {"word": "minds", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "mindeth", "tags": ["archaic", "present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["past", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["plural", "present"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["past", "plural"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["present", "subjunctive"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["past", "subjunctive"]}, {"word": "mind", "tags": ["imperative", "present"]}, {"word": "-", "tags": ["imperative", "past"]}, {"word": "minding", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "minded", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "To bring or recall to mind; to remember; bear or keep in mind.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mind to-morrow's early meeting!", "reference": "1878, Robert Browning, La Saisiaz, line 70:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(now regional) To remember.", "tags": ["regional"], "examples": ["The land where I shall mind you not / Is the land where all's forgot."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete or dialectal) To remind; put one's mind on.", "tags": ["dialectal", "obsolete"], "examples": ["1684-1690, Thomas Burnet, The Sacred Theory of the Earth\nI desire to mind those persons of what Saint Austin hath said."], "quotes": [{"text": "Farewell, kind lord; fight valiantly to-day: / And yet I do thee wrong to mind thee of it, / For thou art framed of the firm truth of valour.", "reference": "1599 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Life of Henry the Fift”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene iii]:"}, {"text": "Let me be punished, that have minded you Of what you should forget.", "reference": "c. 1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Winters Tale”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene ii]:"}, {"text": "This minds me of a cobbling colonel of famous memory.", "reference": "1692, Roger L’Estrange, “ (please specify the fable number.) (please specify the name of the fable.)”, in Fables, of Æsop and Other Eminent Mythologists: […], London: […] R[ichard] Sare, […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "I shall only mind him, that the contrary supposition, if it could be proved, is of little use.", "reference": "1689, John Locke, “Of True and False Ideas”, in An Essay Concerning Human Understanding:"}, {"text": "He minded them of the mutability of all earthly things.", "reference": "1655, Thomas Fuller, The Church-history of Britain; […], London: […] Iohn Williams […], →OCLC, (please specify |book=I to XI):"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To turn one's mind to; to observe; to notice.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Here comes a spirit of his, and to torment me / For bringing wood in slowly. I'll fall flat; / Perchance he will not mind me.", "reference": "1610–1611 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Tempest”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene ii]:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To regard with attention; to treat as of consequence.", "tags": [], "examples": ["It's the worst thing that can ever happen to you in all your life, and you've got to mind it—you've got to mind it. They'll come saying, 'Bear up—trust to time.' No, no; they're wrong. Mind it."], "quotes": [{"text": "Be of the same mind one toward another. Mind not high things, but condescend to men of low estate. Be not wise in your own conceits.", "reference": "1611, The Holy Bible, […] (King James Version), London: […] Robert Barker, […], →OCLC, Romans 12:16:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(chiefly imperative) To pay attention or heed to so as to obey; hence to obey; to make sure, to take care (that).", "tags": ["imperative"], "examples": ["Mind you don't knock that glass over."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "katsoa"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "varoa"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(now rare except in phrases) To pay attention to, in the sense of occupying one's mind with, to heed.", "tags": [], "examples": ["You should mind your own business.", "Upon my coming down, I found all the Children of the Family got about my old Friend, and my Landlady herself, who is a notable prating Gossip, engaged in a Conference with him; being mightily pleased with his stroaking her little Boy upon the Head, and bidding him be a good Child and mind his Book."], "quotes": [{"text": "My lord, you nod: you do not mind the play.", "reference": "c. 1590–1592 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Taming of the Shrew”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene i]:"}, {"text": "Should you ever have a son, Sansa, beat him frequently so he learns to mind you.", "reference": "2000 August 8, George R[aymond] R[ichard] Martin, “Sansa [Stark]”, in A Storm of Swords (A Song of Ice and Fire; 3), London: Voyager, →ISBN, page 68:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To look after, to take care of, especially for a short period of time.", "tags": [], "examples": ["Would you mind my bag for me?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "To be careful about.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Bank Underground Station, London, is built on a curve, leaving a potentially dangerous gap between platform and carriage to trap the unwary. The loudspeaker voice instructs passengers to \"Mind the gap\": the boundary between train and platform.", "reference": "2005, Gillie Bolton, Reflective Practice: Writing And Professional Development, →ISBN, page xv:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "bg", "name": "Bulgarian"}, "word": "пазя се"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "varoa"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(now obsolete outside dialect) To purpose, intend, plan.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "I mind to tell him plainly what I think.", "reference": "c. 1591–1592 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Third Part of Henry the Sixt, […]”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act IV, scene i]:"}, {"text": "[…]and if ever I refused to do his bidding or loitered or took my leisure he beat me with his feet more grievously than if I had been beaten with whips. He ceased not to signal with his hand wherever he was minded to go; so I carried him about the island, like a captive slave, and he bepissed and conskited my shoulders and back, dismounting not night nor day; and whenas he wished to sleep he wound his legs about his neck and leaned back and slept awhile, then arose and beat me; whereupon I sprang up in haste, unable to gainsay him because of the pain he inflicted on me.", "reference": "1885–1888, Richard F[rancis] Burton, transl. and editor, A Plain and Literal Translation of the Arabian Nights’ Entertainments, now Entituled The Book of the Thousand Nights and a Night […], Shammar edition, volume (please specify the volume), [London]: […] Burton Club […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(UK, Ireland) Take note; used to point out an exception or caveat.", "tags": ["Ireland", "UK"], "examples": ["I'm not very healthy. I do eat fruit sometimes, mind."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(originally and chiefly in negative or interrogative constructions) To dislike, to object to; to be bothered by.", "tags": [], "examples": ["I wouldn't mind an ice cream right now.", "Do you mind if I smoke?"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["heed"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mind", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}